00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.590
So brief overview of SRE.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:46.360
Google has eight products with
over a billion users on them.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.770
We manage that with an
SRE team of 2,500 people.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.450
Not that it works
this way, but that's

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.870
over 400,000 users
per operator, which

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:00.650
is pretty crazy economy
of scale and ability

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.240
to get the most out of folks.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.139
Now, the challenge
that the SREs have

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.440
is, how do I keep
everything super reliable?

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.950
Of course, google.com
is synonymous with,

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.730
is my internet up?

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:20.040
But at the same time, Google has
its reputation for innovation.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.540
There's a zillion developers
writing all these applications,

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.456
doing experiments, pushing
things out, pushing

00:02:24.456 --> 00:02:25.210
the envelope.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.450
How do you balance the
need for availability,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.550
and reliability, and
performance with the need

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.950
for moving the business
forward and innovation?

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.360
That's what the SREs are about.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.660
So what have we found
are the ingredients?

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.460
Now, this isn't an SRE talk.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.480
The SREs would do a
full hour on this.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.590
So I apologize to anybody
watching on YouTube later.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:53.160
This is going to be a
very brief overview.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.540
And this is the observations
we found from the Stackdriver

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:57.410
tooling side.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.340
When we observe SREs, what do
we see makes things special?

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.860
First off is a culture.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.890
SREs aren't just running
around with a pager

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:10.130
trying to squash bugs
and react to problems.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.130
It's a very proactive
organization.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.580
They engineer their
reliability into the products.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.910
So pretty much all
the work they do,

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.830
it's frontloaded way before
you ever have an incident.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:27.980
They're automating processes
for monitoring and automated

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:32.650
remediation so that things
hopefully don't get to a page.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.400
They're doing production
readiness reviews

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.650
with the application teams.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.230
Applications can't
go into massive scale

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.970
and SRE take the
pager unless they're

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.770
engineered for liability.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.430
They're setting the principles
of monitoring and alerting.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.320
They're managing the
customer promises,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.364
whether they're internal
or external, the SLOs,

00:03:50.364 --> 00:03:52.530
which you'd think I'm going
to talk about right now,

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.545
but we're going to push that
off a little bit later until we

00:03:54.545 --> 00:03:56.210
talk about the SLO
feature, but that's

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.410
the most important thing.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.880
And then, of course, they do
manage the incident response.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.700
When there is an incident,
very scalable, flexible,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.940
and predictable
process called IMAG,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.690
which you can learn
more about in the book.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:15.950
And they're responsible for the
blameless post-mortem culture

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.769
that we have, which
is really important.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.320
You can't make things
continuously better

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.040
if people aren't comfortable
saying what's gone on,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.700
saying what went wrong if
they're afraid of retribution.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.870
So that's a brief overview
of the SRE culture.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:37.910
Now, for that culture
to work, they need data.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.330
Everything the SREs
do is data-driven.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:41.510
Is it working well?

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:42.200
Is it reliable?

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.980
Is it cost-effective?

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.440
Is it going to scale?

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.114
Without the data, nothing works.

00:04:49.114 --> 00:04:51.530
So the SREs, like I said, we've
got great economy of scale

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:52.080
on that team.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.350
They're outnumbered over
10 to 1 by the developers.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.217
Imagine the SREs went to every
development team and said,

00:04:58.217 --> 00:05:00.050
I want you to put this
metric in this metric

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:02.508
in this metric in this metric,
and put it here, here, here,

00:05:02.508 --> 00:05:03.650
here, and here.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.300
Short conversation, right?

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.290
It's not going to go very well.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:10.910
So what we've done at Google
is we've actually engineered

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:15.080
the SRE practices from
telemetry, at the least,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.430
into the platform itself.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.180
So when a Google engineer
on an application

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:25.370
deploys application code, it's
not just a container or a VM.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.340
It is a service.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.900
It's got a beginning and an end.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.290
It's identifiable.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:32.980
Schedulable as a unit.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:38.780
It's got a discoverable
API with a known contract.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:43.340
And it's got security and quota
management already available

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:44.060
to it.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.250
And it's got the telemetry,
standardized telemetry,

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.770
so that anything
about that service

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.170
is sent back to the
monitoring systems.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.770
So this is pretty cool.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:56.570
So this allows SREs and
the software engineers

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:58.820
to actually be much
more collaborative,

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.860
because we're not pushing
work on one another.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.300
We put the work on
the platform, you

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.070
deploy the code,
everything works.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:09.570
Now, there's a little bit
of oversimplification.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.153
I'm sure Google engineers would
say it's not always that easy.

00:06:12.153 --> 00:06:14.700
But it's a pretty
good approximation.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.390
So now what we've
done is, well, you all

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.010
can all develop on Google.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.480
Or you don't work
at Google, but we

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.220
try to externalize the concepts
that we have into products

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.630
in the open-source.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:29.420
So our container
scheduler, Borg,

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:33.740
is available as
Kubernetes, the concepts.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.570
We're going talk about Istio.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:40.880
That is basically inspired
by one platform, our API

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.070
management system.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:47.240
Our secure RPC system
is externalized as gRPC.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.280
And our instrumentation,
the things

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.770
that actually send the
metrics, traces, and whatnot,

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.095
it's something called census
internally [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:54.095 --> 00:06:55.375
externalized as open census.

00:06:55.375 --> 00:06:56.750
So not only have
we tried to make

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.960
this available for folks to
use on Google, but because it's

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:02.720
open-source, we let you
use it on competing clouds,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.910
let you use it on-prem as well.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.800
So hybrid multi-cloud version of
this opinionated infrastructure

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.300
that will make it
super easy for you

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:15.350
to manage applications as
a collection of services,

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:19.902
rather than a bunch of
infrastructure willy-nilly.

00:07:19.902 --> 00:07:21.860
We're not going to talk
about this much at all,

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.140
but as you can imagine, with
all those engineers writing

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:26.420
all that code, sending all
those standardized metrics,

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.160
we got to put it somewhere.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.800
So we have systems
inside Google that--

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.260
we get petabytes of logs a day.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:38.720
We have billions of
monitored resources, hundreds

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.640
of billions of times series.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.595
And this same system
is actually the basis.

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:45.470
It's the bottom half of
Stackdriver, the part

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:46.820
of Stackdriver you don't see.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.290
So when you use
Stackdriver, you're

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.865
actually storing your
metrics or traces

00:07:50.865 --> 00:07:56.500
in the same kind of system that
Google is storing their own.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:00.480
Lastly, what this presentation
is really about is the tools.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.840
We want to have this
culture change, deal

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.240
with monitoring by
SLO and working off

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.200
of this standardized platform
with these standardized

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:12.020
signals.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.510
Well, to make that
all happen, you

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.460
have to have tools to
bring it all together.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.920
There has to be great
UI and workflows that

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.130
let you see what you've
got, and be able to use it.

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.570
And that's the tip
of the iceberg.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.370
You always see the iceberg,
and the thing on top

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:29.370
is what you see.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.510
So those first three columns,
there's a lot of work in there,

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.820
and we're going to describe
the tip of the iceberg

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:36.611
and show you the tools
that we're building.

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:41.745
So with that, we want
to drill down on Istio.

00:08:41.745 --> 00:08:42.840
Just show of hands.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.230
Who here has heard
of Istio before?

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.040
Fantastic.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.623
I guess that's why
you're in the room.

00:08:48.623 --> 00:08:50.550
Who here has used it before?

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.620
Oh, wow, this is great.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:54.960
So I will do a brief
treatment of Istio,

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.090
because again, this
is not an Istio talk.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:09:03.550
But I do encourage people to go
to the Istio sessions as well.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:08.280
So when people start breaking
up monolithic applications

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.910
into smaller and smaller
microservice applications,

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.760
the prototypical
two-pizza-box team,

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.340
you started creating a
lot more communication

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.830
between a lot more
pieces of software.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.680
And that creates
a lot of problems.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.580
If you had one monolithic
piece of software,

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:28.050
you'd put the security,
and the quota management,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:31.380
and the instrumentation,
you'd just put inside monolith

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.590
and be done with it.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:35.640
But now, if I've got tens or
hundreds of microservices,

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.260
I don't want to do that
again, and again, and again,

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:38.760
and again.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.760
I just want to do that
once and be done with it.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.480
And that's what Istio provides.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:46.620
It provides security for
microservice communication.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:51.780
So as the security becomes
configuration rather than baked

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:55.590
into the code, it provides
operational agility.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:59.430
So you could say, hey, I want
to have this traffic splitting,

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.310
10% go here and 90% go here.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:02.310
I want to do the canary.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:03.393
I want to do the rollback.

00:10:03.393 --> 00:10:06.540
I want to manage how
the traffic flows.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.150
Again, that comes from Istio.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:13.170
And lastly, the
uniform observability.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:17.670
Because Istio is in this
place, this service mesh that's

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:20.400
available to all
services, the mesh

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.010
itself collects the
metrics and traces,

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:27.250
and sends everything to the
monitoring system of choice.

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:30.520
So this service
mesh is what creates

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:35.250
this open system for being
able to deploy code as services

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.550
and manage it through config,
rather than more and more

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:43.170
and more software logic.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:46.600
In terms of the architecture--

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:50.040
so the key thing here
is the Envoy proxy.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:54.050
The Envoy proxy is
what's sitting in

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.220
between service and service.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:00.420
And its where the
metrics for the API

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.610
are captured and
enabled to be sent off.

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.030
The proxy is also
able to call out

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.910
to the mixer for things like
quota checks and security

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:09.930
checks.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:13.957
And the mixer then doubles
also as an aggregation point.

00:11:13.957 --> 00:11:15.540
So instead of having
to have a zillion

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.330
streams coming back to
your monitoring system,

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:23.820
being able to batch those up and
send it back in a sensible way.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:28.350
Lastly, you have Pilot that is
pushing out the configuration

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.870
everywhere so that we can have
as many stateless pieces as

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.070
possible.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:38.610
And Citadel, which is dealing
with the identity and security

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.280
pieces.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.320
So that is a brief overview of
SRE, brief overview of Istio.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.400
Very theoretical to this point.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:51.780
So let's see practically,
how does Tim use

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.890
this to make Descartes better.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:55.260
TIM KELTON: Thanks, Jay.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:03.322 --> 00:12:07.470
So just as Istio is focused
on providing visibility

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:11.820
into your services, and what
is happening between them

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:14.430
and how they interact,
Descartes Labs

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:19.380
is actually focused on improving
visibility and understanding

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:24.600
of changes that happen every
day all over the earth, all

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.510
over the globe, and how those
changes then might impact

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:32.430
our customers, their businesses,
and even their global supply

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:33.360
chains.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:38.100
And we accomplish this through
building of machine learning

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:38.760
models.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:41.850
And those are fused together
with many different types

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:48.450
of geospatial and
remote sensing data,

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:51.480
such as satellite imagery
or aerial imagery.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:56.250
So you can think of that almost
as somewhat of a service level

00:12:56.250 --> 00:13:00.300
indicator of monitoring
changes all over the earth.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.610
And we're a startup that's
actually just a few years old.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:07.470
Our headquarters are up in Santa
Fe, New Mexico, way high up

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:08.580
in the mountains.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.590
And we don't actually
have any data centers.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.530
We don't have any
physical servers.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:17.400
So the cloud has been this
critical component for us

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.310
as far as infrastructure.

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.980
We could have never built
something on our own

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.190
this quickly as just a
couple-year-old startup.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:30.840
And we have nearly 15
petabytes of satellite imagery.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:32.940
And our machine
learning team needs

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.640
to scale models over
tens of thousands

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.750
of cores on any given day.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:42.480
So whenever possible, we really
push to use managed services.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.480
We're heavy users of GKE.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.900
We also use Stackdriver for a
lot of our monitoring, logging,

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:52.740
tracing, all of those
types of things.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:56.780
So how do we build these
service level indicators

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:58.400
for the whole earth?

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:03.110
Well, we do this by building
machine learning models,

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.900
like I said, and pulling
together this imagery.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:09.710
But it's also not just about
having a single source of data.

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:12.980
Most of the time, the
accuracy in our models

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:19.730
comes about from
combining geographic data

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:23.240
across large geographies,
often through many different

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:25.880
spectrums of light,
not just human visible,

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:29.350
things like infrared, near IR.

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:32.240
Sometimes we'll
use remote sensing,

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.910
such as synthetic aperture
radar, or signals like that.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.580
And then it's also
large temporal windows.

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:41.450
It's not just hours or
days, but sometimes we're

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:44.690
actually spanning, and building
models, and testing them back

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.850
over decades.

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:50.910
And no one data set usually
will just be the answer.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:53.450
So in some ways,
this really isn't

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:57.410
that different than
understanding what's going on

00:14:57.410 --> 00:14:59.420
with your microservice.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:02.690
It's often the fusion of data
for many different services,

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:05.390
many different sources
together that give you

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:08.360
a full picture of how the
application is actually

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:10.120
interacting.

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:15.230
And so Descartes Labs model's
pull together internal data.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:20.030
We pull in public data,
lots of imagery from NASA,

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:25.010
or Landsat, also the
European Space Agency.

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:27.360
But we also pull in many
commercial data sets.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:29.090
We have a lot of our own data.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:32.600
But we try and pair
the data with what

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:36.620
will provide the biggest
answers to provide context.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:40.250
And our goal is to scale
up building of models

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:45.440
over massive geographic regions,
over many different modalities,

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:47.420
and over really large
temporal windows

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:50.090
to provide that insight
for our customers.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:53.840
Some examples would
be just last Friday,

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:58.250
we were running models
and looking at building--

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:02.070
looking at hurricanes, and
Florida, and things like that.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.520
And we were able to quickly
scale out, and build models,

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:07.340
and identify every single
building in the whole state

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:10.960
of Florida in just a few hours.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:14.740
So what does our
architecture look like?

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:19.510
Well, we're very GKE-centric
on our microservices.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:23.800
We have around 50 microservices.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:27.520
We have, as I said, around 15
petabytes of earth observation

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:28.930
and sensor data.

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:31.990
Most of that is
actually stored in GCS.

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:34.840
We have some core
key services that

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:38.430
can selectively pull just
parts of the objects from GCS.

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:41.860
It could just pull, for
example, a single band of light,

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:43.730
and just be able to query that.

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:48.970
And we have to be able to scale
those compute jobs individually

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:52.970
to tens of thousands of cores.

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:56.300
And our microservices,
we have we

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.960
use a wide range of
data persistence tools,

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:02.480
besides just the object
store that's on GCS.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:06.040
We impair the microservice
with the appropriate backend.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:08.380
It could be Cloud
SQL, or big table,

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:12.550
or any of those databases.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:15.550
And we leverage a lot of
them for each service.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:17.930
But all of our monitoring,
logging, and tracing

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.619
is then back into Stackdriver.

00:17:20.619 --> 00:17:23.170
So what are some of
the challenges we face

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:26.319
with scaling microservices?

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:28.030
Probably like
everyone here who's

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.670
had a lot of different services,
one of the biggest things

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:32.590
is visibility.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:34.600
Understanding of
the source code,

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:37.210
the components,
what was deployed

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.810
with each of the microservices,
how each of those microservices

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:43.390
then start to interact together.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:47.830
How many times does
a black box tester

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:51.610
maybe use one intended use case,
but then this microservice is

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:54.450
really valuable, and somebody
else finds out about it,

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.310
another developer starts using
it in a totally different way

00:17:57.310 --> 00:18:00.590
that you never intended?

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:04.510
And so in my application
changes, another problem,

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:09.100
how do I quickly
observe what's going on?

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.110
We'll have applications
that are configured

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.510
with a variable maybe
was changed downstream,

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:15.430
and there was a
huge consequence.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:18.910
It didn't show up
in early tests.

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:20.320
Maybe it changed the traffic.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.650
Maybe the service to service
interaction is now different.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:26.600
So if I release a
new version of code,

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.690
how do I gain
understanding about

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:31.630
did this improve my efficiency
on the backend of the service?

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:36.655
So what's really needed?

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:42.940
Prior to ever writing a
service level agreement

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:45.100
for any of our
customers, we really

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.130
have to understand the
performance baselines,

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:50.590
and the behaviors of
the actual application,

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:55.550
and how it's performing in real
world with real load traffic.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:19:03.820
So what matters
or is notable, it

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:06.550
might not make any
difference to the customer.

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:09.220
We might have build
metrics and dashboards,

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:11.520
but the end customer actually
can't see the difference.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.590
It could have problems still.

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:18.320
How do all the components of
the service then work together.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.950
How can I easily track
how many errors are

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:23.530
acceptable for our customers?

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:28.900
When do we need to take a
step back on our development

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:33.070
roadmap, and look at instead
of maybe building new features,

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:36.010
we need to improve
the core stability?

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:39.190
What does the product
manager have to figure out

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:40.450
those types of priorities?

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:48.960
So overall, in some ways,
than when we move to Istio,

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:51.720
this really isn't
super different.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:54.750
And this is awesome.

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:58.530
I am not putting
instrumentation libraries

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:01.500
into every single microservice
that we have out there.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:04.890
There is that approach
and I could do that,

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.292
but we have services
written in a number

00:20:07.292 --> 00:20:08.250
of different languages.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:11.110
And I'm guessing many
of you do as well.

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:14.800
And so with Istio, as
Jay mentioned earlier,

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:17.460
one of the keys is
the Envoy proxy.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:21.510
And we inject the sidecar
proxy in with the microservice,

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:24.210
and that gets deployed, and
you can actually set that up

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:25.540
to happen automatically.

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:29.580
So when those pods get deployed,
the Envoy sidecar proxy

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.770
gets reported in,
and then that metric

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:35.610
starts going into the backend.

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:37.950
So this starts to give
us instant visibility

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.740
into each service,
which services are

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:43.860
calling that specific service.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:47.610
It gives us service-to-service
authorization.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:52.110
We can do things like we might
need to rate limit a service.

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:56.190
For example, I like to use
auth as a really good example.

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:58.830
It's OK if it gets hit five
times a second from one

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.520
pod to another pod, but if
it starts getting hit 20, 30,

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:05.190
40,000 times a second
from pod, there's

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:08.080
probably something fishy that
was going on in that code.

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:10.850
And I would want
to go check that.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:14.130
And overall, now
all of our services

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:17.250
are talking back into the
backend Istio control plane

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:20.960
with the pilot in the mixer,
as Jay was mentioning.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.670
And then we have
Envoy just injected

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.530
into each of those containers.

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:27.330
And so with that--

00:21:30.312 --> 00:21:32.860
JAY JUDKOWITZ: Thanks, Tim.

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:33.800
Thanks so much.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:40.670
So we're going to talk a
little bit about the product

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:43.790
that we built, what we've
done to Stackdriver to be

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:48.890
able to better monitor Istio
with all that data we had.

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:51.560
I won't go into gory, gory
detail, because we're just

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:53.596
going to show you with Tim.

00:21:53.596 --> 00:21:55.220
Next he's going to
come up and show you

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:56.780
what Descartes looks like.

00:21:56.780 --> 00:21:59.960
But there's three problems
we really wanted to solve.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:03.170
First problem is, what
the heck am I running?

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:05.590
What is my service architecture?

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:07.460
I could look at it from
a code perspective.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:10.520
I can look at GitHub and see
all the different source files,

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:16.040
and maybe look at a build system
and see what the artifacts are.

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:17.720
But that doesn't
tell me anything

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:19.370
about the infrastructure.

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:20.765
I can look at the
infrastructure,

00:22:20.765 --> 00:22:23.600
and look at VMs, and
pods, and containers,

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:25.950
and that tells me
what I'm consuming,

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:27.860
but it doesn't tell
me what I'm running.

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:29.990
What I really want is--

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:31.940
how many of you have
had to train a new hire?

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:33.191
What's the first thing you do?

00:22:33.191 --> 00:22:34.523
You got to go to the whiteboard.

00:22:34.523 --> 00:22:36.540
You go to the whiteboard
and you draw a picture.

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:39.060
You say, all right, we
have this thing here,

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:41.390
which does this function
and talks to that thing

00:22:41.390 --> 00:22:43.473
there, which talks to that
function, which depends

00:22:43.473 --> 00:22:45.150
on this thing over here.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:48.080
And it turns out humans
have an incredibly intuitive

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:51.250
understanding of what
makes up a complex system.

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:53.300
And what we wanted to do
was be able to make it

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.175
so you'd have to draw
that on the whiteboard,

00:22:55.175 --> 00:22:57.250
but instead got
that automatically.

00:22:57.250 --> 00:22:59.330
And that's what the
service graph is about.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:05.240
If we had a build up from
random pods and containers,

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:06.560
not so easy.

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.680
But because Istio is
opinionated about where

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:11.810
service starts
and ends, and it's

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:13.940
able to track which services
are talking to which

00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:16.095
other services, it's actually--

00:23:16.095 --> 00:23:17.220
I'm not going to say easy--

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:19.940
but it's certainly a lot
simpler to build this graph.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:21.680
And that's what we've
done is basically

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:25.220
graphed out the entire
Istio service topology.

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:28.110
Now, we've done a couple other
things here that are important.

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:31.860
One is we've added
a time element.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:34.490
So how many have you ever had
to do tech support for somebody

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:36.740
you love or care about?

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:38.600
What's your first question?

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:40.370
What did you change?

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:42.600
And what is their first answer?

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:43.570
Nothing.

00:23:43.570 --> 00:23:45.931
And are they ever
telling the truth?

00:23:45.931 --> 00:23:46.430
No.

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:47.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:51.500
So on the bottom there
is a time slider.

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:53.864
And so that time slider
lets you go back in time.

00:23:53.864 --> 00:23:56.030
And so you can go see, OK,
what did the service look

00:23:56.030 --> 00:23:57.500
like an hour ago?

00:23:57.500 --> 00:23:59.000
What did it look
like two hours ago?

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:01.340
See where the changes come in.

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:03.800
Also, this doesn't replace
all of your dashboards

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:06.489
and everything, but you have
basic metrics for each service.

00:24:06.489 --> 00:24:08.030
I mean, where are
the golden signals?

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:08.840
What do people care about?

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:09.710
What is my traffic?

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:10.709
What are my error rates?

00:24:10.709 --> 00:24:12.890
What are my latencies
of various percentiles?

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:14.510
You get that at a glance.

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:16.010
And that's really,
really important,

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:18.830
because let's say you get a
support call about something

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:21.240
at the top of the tree.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.690
Do you want to go debugging
that thing right away?

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:25.190
Maybe, but maybe not.

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:28.850
Maybe it's actually the thing
it depends on has problems.

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:30.710
So if you say, high
latency on something

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:34.460
up top, and then you look
at its five dependencies,

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:36.726
and you go, oh, wow,
one of those five

00:24:36.726 --> 00:24:38.600
actually has high latency
down at the bottom.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.540
I bet the high latency at the
top is caused by this one thing

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:42.440
down lower.

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:46.820
Then you can go debugging right
at the source of the problem.

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:48.590
So this is an
excellent triage tool,

00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:50.256
because it gives you
an at-a-glance view

00:24:50.256 --> 00:24:52.760
of all this stuff.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.770
And then lastly, it
lets you drill down.

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:57.526
It doesn't end at
just a service list.

00:24:57.526 --> 00:24:59.150
Actually, you can
double click on these

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:03.800
and go down from the workload
level to the deployment,

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:06.800
to the replica set, to
the pod, to the container,

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:09.076
and actually see if it
is an infrastructure

00:25:09.076 --> 00:25:10.700
problem, which pieces
of infrastructure

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:13.730
you're going to want to look at.

00:25:13.730 --> 00:25:16.970
Problem number two, how
is my service behaving?

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:20.750
Well, again, there's a lot
of different fractured points

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:21.992
of views of this.

00:25:21.992 --> 00:25:23.450
You could look at
dashboards, which

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:25.210
show you a lot of
different metrics

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:28.181
about the infrastructure, or
response rates, or something

00:25:28.181 --> 00:25:28.680
like that.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.290
And that will give you one idea.

00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:33.440
Or you can dive deep, deep,
deep into the code itself

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:36.329
and go, OK, what does a
distributed trace look like?

00:25:36.329 --> 00:25:38.870
How is my latency distributed
between different microservices

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:40.269
that make up the app?

00:25:40.269 --> 00:25:42.560
But again, you're not getting
a big picture view of it.

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:43.580
Where do you start with?

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:45.560
How do you build your
conceptual model?

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:49.050
And this is where we're
actually going off to the--

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:55.100
I promised I'd defer the
SLO conversation to here.

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:57.650
What you really want, or at
least what we want Google,

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:02.430
is service level objectives
for each service.

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:05.990
So think about how many
servers and how many containers

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:07.220
are running at Google.

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:11.270
Imagine if every time a CPU
went not, an SRE got paged.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:12.300
That would never work.

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:13.430
That would never scale.

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:16.280
No 400,000 users per
SRE at that model.

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:17.700
So they automate a lot.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:19.340
They automate the auto scaling.

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:22.057
So OK, the CPUs go
hot, you auto scale.

00:26:22.057 --> 00:26:23.390
CPUs go down, everything's fine.

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:25.040
Nobody gets paged.

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:26.525
But you can only
automate so much.

00:26:26.525 --> 00:26:28.400
You automate first thing,
and the next thing,

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.025
and the next thing,
and the next thing.

00:26:30.025 --> 00:26:31.721
And at some point,
you get to something

00:26:31.721 --> 00:26:33.720
that didn't get automated
away, and the customer

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.459
experiences pain.

00:26:35.459 --> 00:26:36.750
That's what we want to measure.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:39.510
We want to learn, when does
the customer have pain, alert

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:41.530
on that, and do something.

00:26:41.530 --> 00:26:45.360
So the first question is, what
is a service level indicator?

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:48.910
What is the thing the customer
notices and cares about?

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:50.800
There could be 100
answers to that question,

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:54.030
but there's two that come up
again and again, particularly

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:55.720
for API-based services.

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:58.220
One is availability.

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:02.040
What percentage of my
responses come back with 200s?

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:07.290
The second thing,
latency, performance.

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:10.770
Let's say a customer needs
300 milliseconds response

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:11.900
time on an API.

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:14.880
OK, what percentage
of API calls come back

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:16.920
with 300 milliseconds or less?

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:18.920
Those are your service
level indicators.

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:22.170
A service level objective is
when you put a target on that,

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:26.640
when you say, I want 99%
200 responses a week,

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:33.960
or I want 99.5% 300 milliseconds
or less response time.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:37.080
The service level objectives
are now the promises

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.150
that we're making to
ourselves about our service.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:40.890
It may be communicated
externally.

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:43.842
It's often not.

00:27:43.842 --> 00:27:46.050
But it's important, because
without those objectives,

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:47.716
really hard to figure
out whether you're

00:27:47.716 --> 00:27:49.020
doing well or not.

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:52.910
Now, you notice,
I didn't say 100%.

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:56.740
If I wanted 100% reliability,
we'd never release anything.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:28:03.060
So when you take 100%
minus the target,

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:04.440
that's your error budget.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:10.680
Your error budget says, how much
slack do I have this period?

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.502
So if your error budget
is really super high,

00:28:13.502 --> 00:28:14.460
you're not spending it.

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:16.894
What happens when your
boss gives you money budget

00:28:16.894 --> 00:28:18.810
and you don't spend it
by the end of the year?

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:20.190
Do you get that money
again the next year?

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:20.690
No.

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:21.670
They take it away.

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:24.180
So budget is there to be spent.

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:28.050
And so if your error budget is
super high all the time, well,

00:28:28.050 --> 00:28:30.385
maybe it's time to
release more code.

00:28:30.385 --> 00:28:32.010
Maybe it's time to
do more experiments.

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:33.690
Maybe it's time to see if
you can get more features out

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:34.740
to your customers,
because if it's

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:36.440
more reliable than
your customer's need,

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:38.660
they prefer features.

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:41.040
What if the error budget
is really, really low?

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:44.640
Well, you may want to hold
off on that big release.

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:47.550
And what if your error
budget is still within limits

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:50.280
but dropping precipitously?

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:52.786
Wake somebody up,
because otherwise, you're

00:28:52.786 --> 00:28:54.910
going to be below and you're
not going to be happy.

00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:58.500
So the error budget is really
how the SREs live and die,

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:01.764
and how they
organize their lives,

00:29:01.764 --> 00:29:03.930
and how they know what they
should and shouldn't do.

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.360
You have a contract with
your internal and external

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:07.350
consumers.

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:13.860
You measure it, and
you work based on what

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:15.900
those measurements show.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:18.054
And then of course,
you get a report.

00:29:18.054 --> 00:29:19.470
At the end, you
can say, OK, did I

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:22.260
meet this SLO for this period?

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:26.250
Now, these SLOs are
pictures from Stackdriver.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:27.780
So one of the
features that we've

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:29.839
got-- so the first feature
was the service graph

00:29:29.839 --> 00:29:30.630
that we showed you.

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.870
Second feature is
this SLO feature.

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:34.620
So both for App Engine,
which isn't really

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:37.980
a part of this talk,
and Istio, we're

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:40.080
making it super easy
to put service level

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:44.140
objectives on your services,
measure them, and get

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:47.250
the graphs automatically, and
then be able to set alerts,

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:51.390
and know when to page
people based on those SLOs.

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:54.130
Problem number three, debugging.

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:56.930
So who here's used
Stackdriver before?

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:59.051
All right.

00:29:59.051 --> 00:30:01.050
If this was a small room,
I'd ask how many parts

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:02.045
you can name.

00:30:02.045 --> 00:30:05.650
There's metrics, and logging,
and traces, and profiler,

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:09.750
and debugger, and dashboards,
and error logging.

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:12.360
I'm probably skipping a couple.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:17.370
It's a pretty broad
product, and they each

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:19.050
have their own screen.

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:20.370
So I can go to a metric screen.

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:21.490
I can go to a log screen.

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:23.910
I can go to a trace screen.

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:27.030
But if I'm debugging a problem
in a particular service,

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.130
I just want to know
everything about that service.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:30.570
Don't show me all
the other stuff.

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:32.340
Show me everything
I care about about

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.920
the service in one place scoped
to the time of the problem.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.269
And that's what we've built
with the service dashboard.

00:30:38.269 --> 00:30:39.810
So the service
dashboard has a number

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:41.393
of elements, which
you're going to see

00:30:41.393 --> 00:30:42.730
in Tim's demo in a second.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:44.970
It's got a timeline
slider up above, which

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:46.860
shows all the relevant events.

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:49.650
You can open and
close the time slider

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:53.100
to get around the events
you care about and around

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:54.390
the time you care about.

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:57.420
And that filters everything else
that we're going to talk about.

00:30:57.420 --> 00:30:59.400
There's events on the
side that give you

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:01.560
more details about
what's been going on.

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:02.930
And then there's these tabs.

00:31:02.930 --> 00:31:05.280
And so the first tab is SLO tab.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:08.220
Pretty important, because
when you're debugging,

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:11.730
you always start from
the broad and go deep.

00:31:11.730 --> 00:31:15.570
So the broad thing is, oh, my
SLO's error budget is dropping.

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:17.010
OK, that's the broadest.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.800
All right, what's the
next level of depth?

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:20.850
Metrics.

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:24.250
So for any given
service, we could say,

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:28.800
here's what the
transaction rate is.

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:31.690
Here's what the latency
distributions are.

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:34.030
And then we let you
slice and dice that.

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:39.250
So oftentimes, a service
doesn't just flat out die.

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:41.110
What usually happens
with a service is,

00:31:41.110 --> 00:31:43.370
some subset of
traffic is broken.

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:46.690
This version, from this
sender, in this location.

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:48.940
You want to be able to
slice and dice the traffic,

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:51.544
and figure out
who's the outlier.

00:31:51.544 --> 00:31:52.960
Who's the subset
of traffic that's

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:55.580
making the overall thing bad?

00:31:55.580 --> 00:32:00.900
And so the service level metrics
about availability and latency

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:01.930
let you do that.

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.930
Now, once you've figured
out what subset of traffic

00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:08.680
you care about, you can
dig into diagnostics.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:09.580
Let's look at traces.

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:11.871
Let's see what the different
percentiles of traces are.

00:32:11.871 --> 00:32:13.690
Let's go into that.

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:15.146
Maybe it's not a performance.

00:32:15.146 --> 00:32:16.520
Maybe it's an
availability thing.

00:32:16.520 --> 00:32:17.978
So we want to look
at error reports

00:32:17.978 --> 00:32:20.489
and see what kind of
stack traces we've got.

00:32:20.489 --> 00:32:22.780
And then there's everybody's
favorite, the least common

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:25.270
denominator of debugging, logs.

00:32:25.270 --> 00:32:28.460
When all else fails,
read the log files.

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:31.870
So with this, we're
able to go, again,

00:32:31.870 --> 00:32:34.000
from the big picture
of the service graph,

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:37.510
say, hey, which service
is likely at fault, down

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:40.320
into that service's
dashboard, find the SLO,

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:43.720
to the service metrics, down
to the individual diagnostic

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:45.220
pieces.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:48.460
Ultimately, we'll also want to
include debugger and profiler,

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:50.390
and launch off into there.

00:32:50.390 --> 00:32:54.740
But you can imagine how
that would add on later.

00:32:54.740 --> 00:33:00.730
So who wants to see Tim show
you the product instead of me

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:03.121
talking and doing screenshots?

00:33:03.121 --> 00:33:03.620
Yeah?

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:04.120
All right.

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:06.604
