00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:01.610
if I knew all my traffic.

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:03.240
But I actually know
there's a problem.

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:07.680
So I want to see,
are all the workloads

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:09.930
hitting this API, the
things I know about,

00:39:09.930 --> 00:39:14.390
or is there actually
something new coming in?

00:39:14.390 --> 00:39:17.280
And then I can scroll
down and I can actually

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:21.570
see that there's a new
service that's hitting.

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:26.570
Some developer got the wild idea
that he knew how to use my API

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:28.710
and he was going to
make this killer app.

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:32.310
And he is basically
crushing that single pod.

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:35.120
So that was never
going to scale,

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:39.200
and my latencies were going
to basically drop off.

00:39:39.200 --> 00:39:42.290
And I can start seeing the
here that the latencies

00:39:42.290 --> 00:39:44.490
are getting slower and slower.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:49.220
So now I've added more
backend Raster pods.

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:54.200
I should be able to
start handling that load.

00:39:54.200 --> 00:39:57.680
And now I might want to go
in and dive even deeper.

00:39:57.680 --> 00:39:59.840
The next step I would
do is I would actually

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:03.630
want to look at the
diagnostics themselves.

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:08.060
So these are some
of the requests,

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:10.370
and the percentile,
and the latency.

00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:12.590
And I can actually
see a sampling

00:40:12.590 --> 00:40:15.230
of what some of those
calls to Raster were.

00:40:15.230 --> 00:40:19.970
I can actually start
going in and seeing,

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:22.040
here's this Raster service.

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:23.840
And I'm seeing
these slower times.

00:40:23.840 --> 00:40:26.960
These were in the 90
and 95th percentile.

00:40:26.960 --> 00:40:31.520
And I can actually start
seeing the full traces.

00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:35.720
One thing to mention,
we've actually--

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:37.880
I can click around
on a few of those.

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:45.950
I can look down at
that time window,

00:40:45.950 --> 00:40:49.490
and I can actually see
some of the logs coming in.

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:52.190
And I can look at--

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:54.110
we're having some
problems, it looks like.

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:57.950
I can see logs relative
to this small time window

00:40:57.950 --> 00:40:59.180
just to this service.

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:01.790
It's already filtered
that down for me.

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:06.389
I didn't actually build any of
these dashboards or widgets.

00:41:06.389 --> 00:41:07.430
So that's really helpful.

00:41:07.430 --> 00:41:09.710
Here I actually see
some good requests.

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:12.530
I can actually see we
served Raster imagery.

00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:17.300
Looks like this was a metadata
request to NASA Landsat scene.

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:20.560
So that's really nice to be
able to compartmentalize that.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:24.790
Oh, and look, I just now am
seeing an incident pop up.

00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:28.940
I am seeing I actually
have an error budget.

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:31.970
I'm losing error budget, and
I now have a new incident.

00:41:31.970 --> 00:41:36.260
So I might want to start
understanding that.

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:43.040
So now at this point, hopefully
we've stopped the bleeding.

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.080
So at this point,
I thought it would

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:47.420
be helpful to just
show actually how

00:41:47.420 --> 00:41:51.560
I make an SLO to begin with.

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:56.180
Since all of my services
are behind Istio,

00:41:56.180 --> 00:42:01.280
I can actually use the service
graph or the service explorer,

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:07.360
and I can actually see all
of those services themselves.

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:16.540
And so I can actually
go find the--

00:42:16.540 --> 00:42:17.780
which service was this?

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:22.010
OK, I am in the Roswell
Kubernetes project.

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:23.960
I'm picking good
New Mexican names.

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:27.200
That's a city in New Mexico,
if you're not familiar.

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:28.850
And I can go look for--

00:42:32.030 --> 00:42:32.990
let's see.

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:34.837
Where is Roswell?

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:44.380
Oh, sorry.

00:42:44.380 --> 00:42:45.970
Oh, I've already selected that.

00:42:45.970 --> 00:42:46.990
I've self-selected.

00:42:46.990 --> 00:42:50.170
But these were all
populated from Istio.

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:54.580
So now that I've populated
that Raster service,

00:42:54.580 --> 00:42:56.830
I can actually add an SLO.

00:42:56.830 --> 00:43:00.610
And I can do an SLO
with two different types

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:02.560
of service level indicators.

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:04.030
One would be availability.

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:06.730
Maybe my application
always has to be up.

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:10.030
That's what's most critical
for our line of business.

00:43:10.030 --> 00:43:12.310
In this case, I just
showed you, we're

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:14.920
serving all these
varying size imagery.

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:21.280
And really what crushes us from
day to day maybe is latency,

00:43:21.280 --> 00:43:23.350
and how quickly
or how responsive

00:43:23.350 --> 00:43:25.250
that service actually is.

00:43:25.250 --> 00:43:27.370
So in this case,
what I'm going to do

00:43:27.370 --> 00:43:32.230
is I'm going to make this
service respond with 4,000

00:43:32.230 --> 00:43:34.240
milliseconds, or four seconds.

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:36.820
And then I can pick a period.

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:39.040
In real life, I
would usually often

00:43:39.040 --> 00:43:42.490
use maybe a month
or at least a week.

00:43:42.490 --> 00:43:44.860
In this case, we're
building a demo,

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:47.510
and I'm just walking
through how we build these.

00:43:47.510 --> 00:43:53.750
So I'm going to actually
select just one day.

00:43:53.750 --> 00:43:57.890
And then finally, I can
pick a target compliance.

00:43:57.890 --> 00:44:02.180
What's the percentage of
traffic that should be served?

00:44:02.180 --> 00:44:07.040
When the latency falls
lower than this amount,

00:44:07.040 --> 00:44:09.890
it starts being a really bad
experience for our customers,

00:44:09.890 --> 00:44:12.450
and we want to do
something about it.

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:14.480
So in this case,
I think I'm going

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:18.330
to try 99.5% of that latency.

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:22.070
And so remember, this
was just over a day.

00:44:22.070 --> 00:44:29.010
So 99% of that traffic, if
I broke 99% up in minutes,

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:31.820
that's about 7 and
1/2 minutes of the day

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:33.900
that the latency can't be below.

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:36.796
So that's actually
probably pretty high.

00:44:36.796 --> 00:44:37.920
It might be too aggressive.

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:38.461
I'm not sure.

00:44:38.461 --> 00:44:40.080
I'm going to actually see.

00:44:40.080 --> 00:44:42.980
Now, this is really nice,
because what's happened

00:44:42.980 --> 00:44:46.310
is they've looked at
the traffic coming in

00:44:46.310 --> 00:44:48.140
between those services.

00:44:48.140 --> 00:44:50.670
And I didn't pre-populate
or pre-build this.

00:44:50.670 --> 00:44:53.150
It's actually pulled
all the request latency.

00:44:53.150 --> 00:44:58.490
And then I can see where I
fit on that 99.5% threshold.

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:01.280
Was that really the right
level to put on a service level

00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:02.570
indicator?

00:45:02.570 --> 00:45:05.690
Actually, I think that
that's not doing too bad.

00:45:05.690 --> 00:45:08.850
I'm looking pretty good there.

00:45:08.850 --> 00:45:12.920
And then I can see what my
error budget would have been.

00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:17.540
And then I can finally see where
my compliance would have been.

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:20.750
And this was a demo cluster.

00:45:20.750 --> 00:45:23.000
We've only been running
load for a couple of days,

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:29.280
so that's actually why this load
stops showing previously there.

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:32.240
So that's just a
walkthrough of how

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:40.100
you can get started building
SLOs once they're behind Istio.

00:45:40.100 --> 00:45:45.920
So at this point, I think I'm
going to check on incidents,

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:50.630
and I'm going to see if
I have any incidents.

00:45:50.630 --> 00:45:54.110
And look, I actually have
budget loss incident.

00:45:54.110 --> 00:45:56.570
It's now been going
for seven minutes.

00:45:56.570 --> 00:45:59.930
If I had set up Stackdriver
paging or alerting based

00:45:59.930 --> 00:46:07.370
on that, that would have started
burning through the error

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:08.180
budget right there.

00:46:10.690 --> 00:46:14.850
So at this point, what I'm going
to do is I'm going to go back

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:20.970
and I'm going to see if
adding all those Raster pods,

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:23.580
did that actually
help anything at all?

00:46:23.580 --> 00:46:27.810
I'm going to expand
the service bar out.

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:29.640
And then I'm going
to look at what's

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:32.500
been happening with my SLO.

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:35.250
And I just want to zoom
in really, really close.

00:46:38.030 --> 00:46:41.660
And it actually looks like
I'm starting to see latency

00:46:41.660 --> 00:46:44.930
now begin to improve.

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:47.960
It reached a bottom, it looked
like it was getting around

00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:50.450
98.15%.

00:46:50.450 --> 00:46:54.710
But now finally, latency
is starting to get better.

00:46:54.710 --> 00:46:56.750
I was burning error budget.

00:46:56.750 --> 00:46:59.880
I'm hoping that that error
budget has now stabilized

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:02.390
and we're starting
to flatten out.

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:05.880
You can see I'm now
out of compliance.

00:47:05.880 --> 00:47:10.100
So hopefully these changes will
start scaling more and more

00:47:10.100 --> 00:47:11.000
pods.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:13.520
And at this point,
we'll start going back

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:15.304
into full compliance.

00:47:20.244 --> 00:47:24.400
And so at this point,
I think we've walked

00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:27.308
through all the major areas.

00:47:27.308 --> 00:47:28.891
JAY JUDKOWITZ: Thank
you so much, Tim.

00:47:28.891 --> 00:47:32.188
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:36.430 --> 00:47:37.610
All right, so, let's see.

00:47:37.610 --> 00:47:39.467
I've got 2 and 1/2 minutes.

00:47:39.467 --> 00:47:40.300
What can I tell you?

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:49.000
So just to recap, SRE
practices are awesome.

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:50.969
Istio is platform
technology that

00:47:50.969 --> 00:47:52.510
helps you get there
so that you don't

00:47:52.510 --> 00:47:54.320
have to build all
the SRE practices

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:56.350
into each microservice yourself.

00:47:56.350 --> 00:48:00.040
Stackdriver automates the
creation of service graph,

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:01.750
automates the creation
of dashboards,

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:04.270
automates the graphing of SLOs.

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:06.490
Like Tim showed, you just
have to supply the target,

00:48:06.490 --> 00:48:08.860
and it takes it from there.

00:48:08.860 --> 00:48:13.300
Hopefully this gives you
the best possible visibility

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:15.880
into your services,
and also gives you

00:48:15.880 --> 00:48:18.850
the best possible mean
time to resolution

00:48:18.850 --> 00:48:21.130
when problems do occur.

00:48:21.130 --> 00:48:22.630
I definitely
encourage you to see

00:48:22.630 --> 00:48:24.670
some of the other talks
that are out there.

00:48:24.670 --> 00:48:27.220
And we're in early
access for the software

00:48:27.220 --> 00:48:28.210
that we showed you.

00:48:28.210 --> 00:48:29.794
It's not generally released yet.

00:48:29.794 --> 00:48:31.210
So if anybody wants
take a picture

00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:34.000
of that link, the
shortened link,

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:37.450
that's the survey to
apply for the software

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:40.975
to get early access.

00:48:40.975 --> 00:48:43.480
Yeah, so thank you very, very
much for your attendance.

00:48:43.480 --> 00:48:44.980
Really appreciate everything.

00:48:44.980 --> 00:48:47.510
And thank you, Tim,
for contributing.

00:48:47.510 --> 00:48:51.160
You would not believe what this
guy went through last week.

00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:53.710
So thank you, everyone, and
enjoy the rest of the show.

00:48:53.710 --> 00:48:55.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:04.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

